  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "b2.travel-and-identity.slow-travel",
      "level": "B2",
      "topics": ["travel-and-identity"],
      "title": "Off the beaten track",
      "text": "Every summer, millions of tourists visit the same famous places, take the same photos and go home. Daniel decided to travel differently. For six months he traveled through small towns, staying with local families instead of in hotels and avoiding the usual sightseeing tours. \"Some famous places are definitely worth visiting,\" he says, \"but the best moments of my trip happened off the beaten track.\" He learned to cook with a grandmother who spoke no English, and he spent a whole afternoon helping a farmer find a lost goat. Traveling this way was not always easy. In some villages nobody understood him, and at times he felt homesick and lonely. But he also discovered how much of his identity came from the place where he grew up: his sense of humor, the food he missed and even his accent. \"You don't really know your own culture until you leave it,\" he says. \"It's worth traveling just for that.\"",
      "source": {
        "kind": "original"
      }
    }
  ],
